        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Clarify `attr()` URL limitations and add `image-set()` example]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41948</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41948"/>
        <updated>2025-11-14T09:45:27.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/44868357?u=096bd31a657e3fc94f4821b3b53e251c7c1d07f6&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">This updates the explanation of how <code class="notranslate">attr()</code> behaves when used in URL-related contexts.<br>
The previous text implied a general restriction, but the real limitation applies <strong>only when the value is required to be a <code class="notranslate">&lt;url&gt;</code> type</strong>.</p>
<p dir="auto">The revised wording explains that:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><code class="notranslate">attr()</code> values are <em>attr-tainted</em>.</li>
<li>Using them in a context requiring a <code class="notranslate">&lt;url&gt;</code> (e.g. inside <code class="notranslate">url()</code> or <code class="notranslate">background-image</code>) results in IACVT.</li>
<li>Some functions—such as <code class="notranslate">image-set()</code>—accept a <code class="notranslate">&lt;string&gt;</code> first, which is later interpreted as a URL. This can allow <code class="notranslate">attr()</code> to work <strong>in shorthand properties like <code class="notranslate">background</code></strong>, although <strong>it does not work in <code class="notranslate">background-image</code></strong>.</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">This update was made after watching a video by <a href="https://space.bilibili.com/31556431" rel="nofollow">zhangxinxu</a> on bilibili:<br>
<a href="https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1dPkzB3Esx" rel="nofollow">https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1dPkzB3Esx</a><br>
I realized the documentation should more accurately reflect the difference between <code class="notranslate">&lt;url&gt;</code>-required contexts and cases where <code class="notranslate">&lt;string&gt;</code> is allowed before URL resolution.</p>]]></content>
